<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Francia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Middle Kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> was the central kingdom created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> was divided among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. It went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> and stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It soon fragmented further, and its northern part, Lotharingia, was contested between East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-francia.jpg" alt="Middle Francia" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maix¿?; derivative work by Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into West Francia under Charles the Bald, <b>Middle Francia</b> (from the Low Countries through Burgundy to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German, ending the unified empire in practice even as the imperial title passed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Verdun of 843 it lay in the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> and then in Lotharingia, a borderland contested by the East and West Frankish kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun in August 843 divided the realm into three kingdoms: West Francia under Charles, East Francia under Louis, and <b>Middle Francia</b>, running from the North Sea to Italy, under Lothar, who kept the imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian objects of the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> found at the site indicate early contact with Egypt as well.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Middle Kingdom</b> grew out of the divided Egypt of the First Intermediate Period, when a dynasty at Herakleopolis claimed the throne in the north and a line of Theban rulers, the Eleventh Dynasty, built up power in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harper's Songs are ancient Egyptian texts that first appear in tomb inscriptions of the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> and were still copied on papyrus in the New Kingdom, as in the Papyrus Harris 500.</blockquote><a href="/songs/harpers-songs">Harper's Songs</a></li></ul></section>
